# generated by Yu'lon — do not hand-edit; the install engine rewrites this file.
#
# WoW Tortoise server image: `tortoise-wow/tortoise-wow` on branch `bot-helpers`
# — the Penqle core, a MaNGOS Zero lineage — with `Sagiroth/TortoiseBots` cloned
# into `modules/TortoiseBots` and compiled in statically, from the ./src tree the
# install engine cloned on the host.
#
# This replaced `Shyalya/tortoise-wow` branch `playerbots-integration-gh`, which
# vendored the cmangos playerbots at `modules/mod-playerbots` and is retired. The
# whole of that change was measured before it was made, on `yulon-arch`
# 2026-09-11, and the evidence is `pyplan/gates/t30-measure-yulon-arch-2026-09-11/`:
# every claim below cites a file in it.
#
# The apt list and the two `FROM` lines are unchanged from the fork's template.
# The `-dev` packages are deliberately in BOTH stages, because in the script this
# template replaced the compiling image WAS the running image, so every one of
# them is also a runtime dependency of mangosd.
#
# Three things the runtime keeps besides the binaries, and the third is new:
#   * sql/ at {{CORE_DIR}}/sql — mangosd's `Database.AutoUpdate.Path` (set to
#     exactly that path by this entry's conf table) reads it at first boot.
#     Measured: 144 world and 1 character migration applied there, no failure
#     (`08b-autoupdater-run1.txt`).
#   * tools/mmap at {{CORE_DIR}}/src/tools/mmap — MoveMapGen's off-mesh and
#     settings inputs, named by the mmaps argv in catalog.json. NOTE the source
#     side moved: the whole tools tree is TOP-LEVEL `tools/` on this core where
#     the fork kept it under `src/tools/` (`02-layout.txt`), and the `cp` below
#     spells the new one. The destination is unchanged, because the catalog's
#     argv names it.
#   * /src/modules — a symlink, and the reason is finding 3 of Half 1. See below.
FROM ubuntu:24.04 AS builder

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
ENV TZ=UTC

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    build-essential cmake git pkg-config ca-certificates \
    libace-dev default-libmysqlclient-dev libmysqlclient-dev \
    libssl-dev zlib1g-dev libbz2-dev \
    libboost-thread-dev libboost-filesystem-dev libboost-system-dev \
    mariadb-client gosu \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

COPY src/tortoise-wow /src

# **A clone made from inside an `ubuntu:22.04` image over HTTP/2 fails**, and
# this line is here because of it — but the diagnosis was made on the OTHER two
# entries, not on this one. In `cmangos/mangos-tbc` and `cmangos/mangos-classic`
# CMake's `FetchContent_MakeAvailable(zlib)` in `dep/src/CMakeLists.txt` clones
# https://github.com/madler/zlib.git at CONFIGURE time, so THOSE builds reach
# the network from inside the container before a single file is compiled. That
# is the TBC/Vanilla reason and it does not apply here: this core's
# `dep/src/CMakeLists.txt` is the old MaNGOS file with no FetchContent in it,
# and the configure-only probe on `yulon-arch` 2026-09-11 reached the end of
# CMake with no network at all (`03-cmake-probe-old-flags.txt`). The line stays
# anyway: it costs nothing, it covers whatever git this build or a later branch
# does run, and three templates that say the same thing are worth more than one
# that has to be remembered as the exception.
#
# Measured on `yulon-ubuntu` 2026-09-02, on the first WoW Vanilla install: the
# clone died three times with `fatal: could not read Username for
# 'https://github.com': No such device or address` / `fatal: expected flush
# after ref listing`, and cmake stopped at `Build step for zlib failed: 2`
# after 202s. Not a credential problem and not the network: `git ls-remote`
# from `alpine/git` on the same box SUCCEEDED, the same command from
# `ubuntu:22.04` FAILED, and the same command from `ubuntu:22.04` with
# `-c http.version=HTTP/1.1` SUCCEEDED. The HTTP/2 conversation is what breaks.
#
# `--system` rather than `--global`: it lands in `/etc/gitconfig` and so applies
# to whatever user cmake ends up running git as, not only to root's HOME.
RUN git config --system http.version HTTP/1.1

WORKDIR /src/_build
# THE FLAG LINE, and the three flags that had to go.
#
# `-DMODULES=static -DMODULE_TORTOISEBOTS=static` is what builds the bots. CMake
# printed, on the build this image is copied from (`04-build.log`):
#
#     -- Build modules         : static
#     --   TortoiseBots: static (MODULE_TORTOISEBOTS=static)
#     -- TortoiseBots source: /src/modules/TortoiseBots commit fd7ec9ec… (clean)
#
# `MODULES` still defaults to `disabled` on this core, so the module must be
# named — globally, per module, or both, and both is what the module's own
# README §2 asks for.
#
# **`BUILD_PLAYERBOTS`, `MODULE_MOD_PLAYERBOTS` and `MODULE_MOD_DUNGEON_CLEAR`
# are gone and must not come back.** They were the fork's, and this core does
# not know them. Run against it on 2026-09-11 (`03-cmake-probe-old-flags.txt`)
# the old line configured to completion and merely WARNED:
#
#     CMake Warning:
#       Manually-specified variables were not used by the project:
#         BUILD_PLAYERBOTS
#         MODULE_MOD_DUNGEON_CLEAR
#         MODULE_MOD_PLAYERBOTS
#
# A warning, not an error — so the old line still produces an image, and that
# image is a BOT-LESS SERVER THAT BOOTS AND LOOKS ENTIRELY HEALTHY (CMake prints
# `TortoiseBots: disabled (MODULE_TORTOISEBOTS=default)` and nothing else
# complains, ever). That is the same trap the fork's own template carried a
# comment about, one core later.
#
# The other six are still real options of this core's `CMakeLists.txt`, read at
# `:38` `USE_STD_MALLOC`, `:40` `USE_ANTICHEAT`, `:42` `USE_SCRIPTS`, `:43`
# `MODULES`, `:44` `USE_EXTRACTORS`, `:52` `ALLOW_TURTLE_ADDONS`, `:57`
# `DEBUG_SYMBOLS` (`02-layout.txt`). DEBUG_SYMBOLS=OFF slims mangosd;
# USE_ANTICHEAT=OFF is pointless on a solo offline server and can flag a Proton
# client.
#
# Both `FROM` lines are ubuntu:24.04 and they move together. That was decided
# for the fork on 2026-09-08 because its vendored gsoap archive imposed a
# glibc 2.38 floor on the RUNTIME stage as well as the builder; this core has no
# gsoap at all, but the pin was built and booted on 24.04 on 2026-09-11 and a
# base-image change is a thing to measure, not to tidy.
RUN cmake .. \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX={{CORE_DIR}} \
        -DUSE_EXTRACTORS=ON -DUSE_SCRIPTS=ON -DUSE_STD_MALLOC=ON \
        -DDEBUG_SYMBOLS=OFF -DUSE_ANTICHEAT=OFF -DALLOW_TURTLE_ADDONS=ON \
        -DMODULES=static \
        -DMODULE_TORTOISEBOTS=static

RUN make -j{{MAKE_JOBS}} && make install

RUN mkdir -p {{CORE_DIR}}/sql {{CORE_DIR}}/src/tools && \
    cp -r /src/sql/. {{CORE_DIR}}/sql/ && \
    cp -r /src/tools/mmap {{CORE_DIR}}/src/tools/mmap

# ONE of the fork's migrations could not apply to its own base, and this is the
# rewrite for it. Measured on m910q 2026-09-08 on a copy of a live install
# (pyplan/gates/tortoise-reimport-rehearsal-m910q-2026-09-08/): with tw_world
# imported from sql/base at the fork's head, the updater applied 157 world
# migrations and cancelled the server on 20260903063722_world with
#   [1062] Duplicate entry '44070' for key 'PRIMARY'   (spell_proc_event)
#
# ON THIS CORE THE FILE APPLIES CLEANLY AND THIS LINE CHANGES NOTHING, measured
# 2026-09-11: the file is still there and still has its 14 plain `INSERT INTO`
# (`06-sql-layout.txt`), but this tree's `sql/base/` does not carry the 44070
# row, and a fresh import ran all 144 world migrations with no failure
# (`08b-autoupdater-run1.txt`). It is kept anyway and deliberately. The updater
# is hash-keyed (SHA1 of the file), so the rewritten copy is a distinct
# migration to it either way; and the one thing this line costs — `INSERT
# IGNORE` skipping a row that would otherwise collide — is exactly what an
# install that reaches this file from the OTHER direction needs. Deleting it is
# a decision to make when upstream fixes the file, not while moving cores.
RUN sed -i 's/^\(\s*\)INSERT INTO/\1INSERT IGNORE INTO/' \
    {{CORE_DIR}}/sql/database_updates/world/20260903063722_world.sql

FROM ubuntu:24.04

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
ENV TZ=UTC

# The compiler and the build helpers are dropped; every library the script
# installed stays, because the image it installed them into is the one that ran
# mangosd, realmd and the extractors.
#
# These are the `-dev` packages, not the lean versioned runtime ones TBC and
# Vanilla use beside this file (`libboost-system1.74.0` and friends). That is
# deliberate for now and it is not free: the `-dev` chain drags in headers,
# static libraries and pkg-config nobody runs. It stays until a Tortoise gate
# can prove a trimmed runtime image still starts, because a runtime package
# named wrongly here does not fail the build — it fails `./mangosd` forty
# minutes later, on a machine nobody is watching. Trim it with a gate, not with
# a guess.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    libace-dev default-libmysqlclient-dev libmysqlclient-dev \
    libssl-dev zlib1g-dev libbz2-dev \
    libboost-thread-dev libboost-filesystem-dev libboost-system-dev \
    mariadb-client gosu ca-certificates \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# The whole prefix: bin/mangosd, bin/realmd, the extractors beside them, the
# etc/ confs the conf stage copies out, plus the sql/ and src/tools/mmap trees
# the two settings above point at, and modules/TortoiseBots/data/sql/, which
# `make install` also laid down there.
COPY --from=builder {{CORE_DIR}} {{CORE_DIR}}

# THE MODULE'S SQL, and the one line that decides whether a fresh install starts
# at all. Finding 3 of T30 Half 1, measured twice on `yulon-arch` 2026-09-11.
#
# The core applies a module's migrations from
#   <modulesPath>/<module>/data/sql/<CharUpdateName|WorldUpdateName>
# (`src/shared/Database/AutoUpdater.cpp:245-283`), and `modulesPath` is
# `TW_SOURCE_MODULES_DIR`, compiled in as `${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/modules`
# (`CMakeLists.txt:548`, `AutoUpdater.cpp:503-506`) — that is `/src/modules`
# here, in the BUILDER's source tree, which the runtime stage does not have. A
# module folder that is not there is skipped in silence (`:268-270`).
#
# So run 1 — this image, nothing mounted — applied every core migration, found
# the module's `world` folder through a source tree that happened to still be
# around, found no `character` folder at all, and died before its ready line:
#
#     [1146] Table 'tw_char.ai_playerbot_equip_cache' doesn't exist
#     … RandomItemMgr::BuildEquipCache … TortoiseBots::BotHostAdapter::OnStartup
#
# (`08-world-run1.txt`). Run 2 changed one thing and the same image came up
# (`08d`, `08e`): 5 character and 3 world module migrations applied.
#
# THE FIX IS THIS SYMLINK, and the alternative was rejected on purpose.
#
#   * The alternative was to rename `data/sql/char` to `data/sql/character` in
#     the source tree before the build and carry that tree into the runtime
#     image. It works, and it puts OUR spelling of the module's folder names
#     into this file — where it would go on being right only for as long as the
#     module keeps its current layout, silently and with the run-1 crash as the
#     symptom when it stops.
#   * A conf key for the folder name is not available: the name the updater
#     looks for IS `Database.AutoUpdate.CharUpdateName`, the same key the core
#     uses for its own `sql/database_updates/character`. Setting it to `char`
#     to suit the module would point the core's own character migrations at a
#     directory that does not exist.
#
# `TortoiseBots.cmake:61-69` already does the rename at `make install`, into
# `{{CORE_DIR}}/modules/TortoiseBots/data/sql/character`, with a comment saying
# it does so "so a fresh install cannot silently skip SQL" — the module owns
# that decision and ships it. Pointing the compiled-in path at the INSTALLED
# tree is therefore the version that survives a module update: when the module
# renames a folder, adds one, or drops one, its own CMake moves with it and
# this line does not have to.
RUN mkdir -p /src && ln -sfn {{CORE_DIR}}/modules /src/modules

WORKDIR {{CORE_DIR}}/bin

# Overridden by the compose overlay for realmd; both services share this image.
CMD ["./mangosd"]
